        "answer": {
          "short": "i. $y$: $\\frac14,\\frac12,1,2,4$.\n\nii. Increasing exponential curve through $(0,1)$, approaching the $x$-axis on the left; see sketch.\n\niii. Domain: $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. Range: $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "Substitution gives\n$$\\begin{align*}y(-2)&=2^{-2}=\\frac14\\\\y(-1)&=2^{-1}=\\frac12\\\\y(0)&=2^0=1\\\\y(1)&=2^1=2\\\\y(2)&=2^2=4\\end{align*}$$\nPlot $(-2,\\frac14),(-1,\\frac12),(0,1),(1,2),(2,4)$ and join them with a smooth increasing exponential curve. Continue the curve in both directions, approaching but never reaching the $x$-axis on the left. Every real input is allowed, and the outputs are all positive. Thus the domain is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $(0,\\infty)$.",
